      "the year under review saw steady execution across the company's business units. your directors remain focused on disciplined bidding, timely certification of running-account bills and recovery of retention and statutory receivables. \n\n### management's discussion — operating review \n\nthe financial year under review was executed against an order book that the board considers adequate for the near term. revenue is recognised on the percentage-of-completion basis across the contract portfolio, and the movement in turnover between the comparative periods reflects the stage of execution reached on the larger packages rather than any change in the commercial terms on which work is taken. \n\nmargins remained within the range the board regards as sustainable for this class of work. input costs — principally cement, steel and bitumen — continued to move with the published indices, and where a contract carries a price-variation clause the recovery has been billed in the normal course. where it does not, the exposure was absorbed within the tendered rates. \n\nreceivables continue to be dominated by government and public-sector clients, whose payment cycles are longer than those of private counterparties but whose credit risk the board assesses as low. retention money is released progressively on expiry of the defect liability periods, and no material amount is regarded as doubtful of recovery at the balance-sheet date. \n\nthe company met its statutory obligations in respect of goods and services tax, tax deducted at source and provident-fund contributions during the year, and holds current registrations for every jurisdiction in which it operates. \n\n### state of affairs & order book \n\n17 contracts remained in execution as at year end with an aggregate awarded value of rs. 56796.04 lakh (including approved variations of rs. 13823.06 lakh across 75 variation orders). 7 credit notes aggregating rs. 160.09 lakh were absorbed in revenue. \n\npage 2 of 18 \n\ndoc-ar-2024 \n\n## management discussion & analysis \n\n### segment commentary \n\nthe segmental analysis below follows the internal work-category reporting used by management to allocate resources. movements between categories year on year reflect the natural completion cycle of the underlying packages rather than a deliberate change in the company's market focus; a single large package reaching substantial completion can move a category materially in either direction. \n\n### segment revenue (net) \n\nthe following table records revenue by work category, net of credit notes, reconciled against the underlying vouchers before issue.": "Segment",
      "### quarterly net revenue \n\nthe table below sets out the distribution of revenue across the four quarters, as certified by the officers named at the foot of this document.": "Quarter",
      "### client concentration \n\nthe board reviews client concentration at each meeting. a significant share of billings arises from a limited number of public-sector authorities, which is characteristic of the sector and of the tendering \n\npage 3 of 18 \n\ndoc-ar-2024 \n\nprocess through which work is secured. the board does not consider this a concentration of credit risk, but it does keep the position under review and continues to tender across a spread of authorities and geographies. \n\n### principal clients (by billings) \n\nthe following table records the clients accounting for the largest share of billings, as certified by the officers named at the foot of this document.": "Client",
      "order inflow remained healthy across roads, water and building segments. margins were protected through procurement discipline and recovery-focused billing. \n\npage 4 of 18 \n\ndoc-ar-2024 \n\nbalance sheet as at 31 march 2025": "EQUITY AND LIABILITIES",
